<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>CompressionWorkbench.FileFormat.Ico</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      ICO/CUR byte parsing and writing lives in PNGCrushCS. This project keeps only the archive
      view of an icon bundle: the display name an entry is listed under, and the decision to hand
      an entry to the caller as a file a viewer will open rather than as the bare payload.

      Its compile assets are private to this bridge assembly, as in FileFormat.Gif and
      FileFormat.PngCrushAdapters. The package carries support assemblies whose namespaces overlap
      CompressionWorkbench's own — Compression.Core.BitIO.BitOrder, and the archive package's
      FitsHdu, NiftiReader, WadEntry and Wad2Entry all collide — so the reference has to stop at
      an assembly boundary that does not also contain those. Runtime assets still flow onward.
    -->
    <!--
      1.0.0.750 is the version PNGCrushCS's next release will publish, and is not on nuget.org
      yet: release.yml is dispatch-only and has not been run since Hawkynt/PNGCrushCS#424 landed.
      Until it is, this project cannot restore, and that is the intended state rather than a
      defect.

      The number is not a guess. version.pl stamps MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH from Directory.Build.props
      with the commit count of the declaring folder appended, and version.pl in list mode, run against
      PNGCrushCS main at b3995255 reports 1.0.0.750 for this package. That repository has no open
      pull requests, so nothing can move the count before the release is cut.

      It has to be the exact published version rather than a lower one. A bare version string is
      a floor to NuGet, but this repository promotes NU1603 to an error, so a pin that resolves
      upward fails the build just as surely as one that resolves to nothing.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Hawkynt.FileFormats.Images" Version="1.0.0.750" PrivateAssets="compile" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
